// Room, and how wide a thing is.
//
// The two things every container here needs and neither of which the language
// has a word for. They were written out again in each of the five files that
// wanted them, back when a name declared in one file did not reach another;
// they are written once now.

// Room that outlives the frame asking for it.
//
// `%symbol` puts a Forte declaration under a name something else defines, and
// `__rt_alloc` is `mir::runtime`'s name for the runtime's untraced allocator.
// It is deliberately not `__rt_gc_alloc`: that one takes a type descriptor
// beside the size so the collector can scan what it hands back, and nothing in
// the language can spell one. So what comes back is room the collector does
// not walk into, and a container of things holding pointers keeps nothing
// alive.
//
// Nothing here frees. What the collector owns it takes back on its own, and a
// container that had to be told when to would be the only thing in the
// language that did.
%symbol("__rt_alloc")
pub fn room(bytes: i64): ptr u8

// How far apart two of them stand.
//
// There is no `size_of`, so it is measured rather than asked for: §3 says a
// pointer is indexed by the stride of what it points at, so the difference
// between the addresses of `p[0]` and `p[1]` *is* the stride. Three
// instructions, and right for every `T` a monomorphisation ever produces --
// which is more than a hand-written table of widths would be.
//
// A size and a stride are not the same number. A three-byte element with a
// four-byte alignment strides four, and the stride is the one a container
// wants: it is what the room has to be multiplied by for element `i` to land
// where `p[i]` will look for it.
//
// Nothing is read through the pointer, so a `0 as ptr T` measures as well as a
// real one -- which is what lets a container work out how much room to ask for
// before it has any.
pub fn stride<T>(p: ptr T): i64 {
    unsafe let a = addr p[0] as i64
    unsafe let b = addr p[1] as i64
    b - a
}
